WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.595
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Hi, and welcome
to Building a Virtual Post

00:00:02.595 --> 00:00:03.990
Production Suite.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.760
My name is Adrian
Graham, and I'm

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.010
a solutions architect for
Media and Entertainment

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.900
at Google Cloud.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.660
Today, I want to talk about
content creation workflows

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.580
and how Google Cloud's
products can support

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.160
these types of workloads for
a creative workforce that

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.660
spans the world.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.360
We're going to look at how
artists such as designers,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.340
animators, or editors
can collaborate

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.700
across vast distances using a
solution that takes advantage

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.490
of Google's global
private fiber network

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.970
to reduce latency and improve
the overall user experience.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.950
Content creation
is a global market.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.020
And access to talent is
essential for finding

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.450
the right artist for the
job, even if they live

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.920
on the other side of the globe.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.420
Companies who want
to take advantage

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.040
of regional
production tax credits

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.350
need to hire talent
in these locations.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.020
But building a new physical
facility to house these artists

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.060
can be cost and
time prohibitive.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.000
It doesn't make sense to build
a new facility in a new location

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.790
for every new production.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.250
But on Google Cloud,
it's possible.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:00.750
Today, I'm going to show you a
virtual post-production suite

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.420
that allows artists to work
with their favorite tools,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.050
but with the added flexibility
and power of Google Cloud.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.480
Consider the main components
of a virtual post-production

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.020
suite--

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.260
people such as artists,
producers, and engineers;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.530
virtual workstations to
create and manage content;

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.260
shared storage so
users can collaborate;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.140
compute clusters for
rendering or transcoding;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.050
media asset management systems
and databases to keep track

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.630
of assets; and
archive and disaster

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.900
recovery to ensure durability
and to properly store data.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.520
With a virtual
post-production suite,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.290
all members of a
content creation team

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.260
can access the same assets
and resources regardless

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.610
of physical location.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.350
Cloud native features such
as data encryption, identity

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.920
access management, and
multi-factor authentication

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.920
ensure high-value content
is secure and available only

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.780
to those granted access.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.540
Google Cloud is authorized
by major Hollywood studios

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.460
to host tier-one content.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.770
We work with international
security evaluators

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.530
and other third-party
auditors to ensure compliance

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:02.460
with security best practices,
local laws, and regulations.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.710
To build a virtual
post-production suite on Google

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.690
Cloud, we need three things--

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.720
a client so artists can connect
from their local workstation,

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.690
laptop, or dedicated hardware
device, a broker that

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.000
authenticates users with
an identity service,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.040
such as G Suite or
Active Directory.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.630
Once authenticated,
the user traffic

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.717
is then routed to their
assigned resource.

00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:22.800
The artist is then connected
to their workstation.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.400
Here, artists can access common
resources such as shared file

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.540
systems, asset management
databases, archive workflows,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.460
and other cloud-based
resources to automate

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.890
tasks such as
transcoding or rendering.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.690
To connect to our
workstations, we're

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.150
going to use PC
over IP technology--

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.270
a remote display
protocol from Teradici

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:45.060
PC over IP technology empowers
artists, editors, and engineers

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.120
to display lossless, high
resolution, high frame rate

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.670
images and video across
multiple monitors

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.740
on a variety of
endpoint devices,

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.460
from laptops to highly secure
stateless PC over IP Zero

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.630
Clients.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.880
Using Teradici Cloud
Access software,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.305
which is built on this
PC over IP technology,

00:03:02.305 --> 00:03:04.680
I can connect to a workstation
running in the Los Angeles

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:05.730
region.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.580
Once authenticated, I'm logged
into my virtual workstation

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.360
running Windows 2019 server
with Windows desktop experience.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.940
This is an NVIDIA Quadro
virtual workstation

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.680
using an NVIDIA
P4 GPU, as well as

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.890
Teradici Cloud Access software.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.350
On my workstation, I can
open an existing project

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.030
in Adobe Premiere Pro.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.430
According to
GCPing.com, my latency

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.080
to this workstation
in the LA region

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.190
is only around 20 milliseconds.

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.700
So the experience feels
like a local workstation.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.490
Audio is synchronized properly,
and I can scrub the timeline

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.763
without lag or delay.

00:03:36.763 --> 00:03:39.180
I'm going to upload some footage
from my local workstation

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:40.560
to the cloud volume.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.860
To do this, I'm using
IBM Aspera on cloud.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.400
Once uploaded, I can move this
footage into my cloud volume.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.890
I could also use other
industry-standard file transfer

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.980
tools, such as Signiant
or even Drive File Stream

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.750
for ingesting footage.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.580
I'm going to add this
footage to my edit

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.620
and drop it into the timeline.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.120
Then I'm going to
pass the modified

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.220
project over to another editor.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.050
Let's bring in my
colleague, [? Marku, ?]

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.170
a solutions architect
who is based in Tokyo.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.920
He's going to make
some additional changes

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.200
to this edit, then send it
back to me for rendering.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.530
[? MARKU: ?] Hey, thanks, Adrian
OK, on my cloud workstation,

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.360
I can see the edits
that you made.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.339
I can see the exact
same project data

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.742
because we are using the
NetApp Global File Cache.

00:04:19.742 --> 00:04:21.450
And when I opened the
shared Premiere Pro

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.300
Project, any assets
or files that changed

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.950
will be read across
Google's network

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.560
and cached locally
to the Tokyo region.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.720
I'm going to add my own
footage from the game using

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.870
the same technique
that Adrian did.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:34.600
And once this
footage is available,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:35.730
I will add it to the edit.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.615
I saved my changes
to the team project.

00:04:40.615 --> 00:04:44.832
And now, Adrian, you
should get a notification.

00:04:44.832 --> 00:04:46.290
ADRIAN GRAHAM:
Thanks, [? Marku. ?]

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.990
I see those changes almost
immediately on my workstation.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.075
And the footage you uploaded
is coming across now.

00:04:51.075 --> 00:04:52.770
[? MARKU: ?] All
right, thanks, Adrian.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:53.640
See you later.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.265
ADRIAN GRAHAM: I
could render this edit

00:04:55.265 --> 00:04:57.990
on my virtual workstation on a
separate server running Adobe

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.350
Media Encoder or using any
one of our partner transcoding

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.330
solutions on Google Cloud.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:06.633
For this project, I'm rendering
the video on another instance.

00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:08.550
When the render is done,
I can view the result

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.950
on my virtual workstation
and play back the videos

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.150
I generated at full resolution.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.730
This is a relatively
simple example

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.890
of a virtual
post-production suite.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.830
We didn't cover asset ingestion
or archive and disaster

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:20.830
recovery.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.520
We also didn't explore how
intelligent content discovery

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.520
can help content creators
understand, organize,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.010
and monetize their
assets on Google Cloud.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.610
This demo showed you connecting
to and performing editing tasks

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.770
on a cloud workstation,
uploading footage

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.990
from a local workstation
for use in my edit,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.540
collaboration with others
sharing my project anywhere

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.420
in the world, and how data
can be securely synchronized

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.450
across Google Cloud regions.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.200
There's a lot of new
and exciting projects

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.200
we're working on to take
media and entertainment

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.570
workloads to the next level.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.200
Check the documentation to learn
more, and thanks for watching.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

